it's a **git worktree** on an orphan branch (`kspec-meta`).\n    73→\n    74→```\n    75→.kspec/.git → file pointing to worktree\n    76→  ↓\n    77→gitdir: .git/worktrees/-kspec\n    78→  ↓\n    79→Shadow branch (kspec-meta): orphan branch with spec/task files\n    80→```\n    81→\n    82→**Why:** Spec/task changes don't clutter main branch history. Code PRs and spec changes tracked independently.\n    83→\n    84→**How it works:** Every `kspec` command auto-commits to `kspec-meta`. Auto-pushes to remote if tracking configured. Main branch gitignores `.kspec/`.\n    85→\n    86→**CRITICAL: Always run kspec from project root, never from inside `.kspec/`.** If you see \"Cannot run kspec from inside .kspec/ directory\", check `pwd`.\n    87→\n    88→### Shadow Branch Commands\n    89→\n    90→```bash\n    91→kspec shadow status   # Verify health\n    92→kspec shadow repair   # Fix broken worktree\n    93→kspec shadow sync     # Sync with remote\n    94→```\n    95→\n    96→### Troubleshooting\n    97→\n    98→| Issue | Fix |\n    99→|-------|-----|\n   100→| `.kspec/` doesn't exist | `kspec init` |\n   101→| Worktree disconnected | `kspec shadow repair` |\n   102→| Sync conflicts | `kspec shadow resolve` |\n   103→| Commands seem broken | Check `pwd` — must be project root |\n   104→\n   105→## Key Concepts\n   106→\n   107→### IDs and References\n   108→\n   109→Every item has a ULID (canonical) and slug (human-friendly). References use `@` prefix: `@task-slug` or `@01JHNKAB`.\n   110→\n   111→### Spec Items vs Tasks\n   112→\n   113→- **Spec items** (`.kspec/modules/*.yaml`): Define WHAT to build\n   114→- **Tasks** (`.kspec/project.tasks.yaml`): Track the WORK of building\n   115→\n   116→Tasks reference specs via `spec_ref`. They don't duplicate spec content.\n   117→\n   118→### Task States\n   119→\n   120→```\n   121→pending → in_progress → pending_review → completed\n   122→              ↓              ↓\n   123→          blocked ←──────────┘\n   124→              ↓\n   125→          cancelled\n   126→```\n   127→\n   128→See `kspec help task` for transition commands and options.\n   129→\n   130→## Spec-First Development\n   131→\n   132→**Core principle**: If you're changing behavior and the spec doesn't cover it, update the spec first.\n   133→\n   134→| Situation | Flow |\n   135→|-----------|------|\n   136→| Clear behavior change | Check spec → Update/create spec → Derive task |\n   137→| Vague idea, unclear scope | Capture in inbox → Triage later |\n   138→| Infra/internal (no user impact) | Create task directly, no spec needed |\n   139→| Bug revealing spec gap | Fix bug → Update spec to match reality |\n   140→\n   141→### Plan Mode Workflow\n   142→\n   143→When a plan is approved, you MUST translate it to specs before implementing:\n   144→\n   145→1. Create spec item: `kspec item add --under @parent --title \"Feature\" --type feature`\n   146→2. Add acceptance criteria: `kspec item ac add @spec --given \"...\" --when \"...\" --then \"...\"`\n   147→3. Derive task: `kspec derive @spec`\n   148→4. Add implementation notes to task\n   149→5. Begin implementation\n   150→\n   151→**Plans without specs are incomplete.** The spec with ACs IS the durable artifact.\n   152→\n   153→### Creating Work\n   154→\n   155→- **Clear scope?** → Create task directly\n   156→- **Unclear scope?** → `kspec inbox add \"idea\"` → triage later with `/triage`\n   157→- **Learning/friction?** → `kspec meta observe friction \"...\"` → review with `/reflect`\n   158→\n   159→## Staying Aligned During Work\n   160→\n   161→**Watch for scope expansion:**\n   162→- Modifying files outside your current task\n   163→- Adding functionality the spec doesn't mention\n   164→- \"While I'm here, I should also...\" thoughts\n   165→\n   166→**When you notice something outside your task:** Capture it separately (inbox item, new task, or observation). Add a note to your current task documenting what you found. Don't fix it inline — even small detours compound into drift. Stay on your task.\n   167→\n   168→## PR Workflow\n   169→\n   170→Before creating a PR, mark the task: `kspec task submit @ref` (transitions to `pending_review`).\n   171→\n   172→The full PR lifecycle has three steps — **all required, in order:**\n   173→\n   174→1. **`/local-review`** — Quality gates: AC coverage, test quality, test isolation. Run this FIRST.\n   175→2. **`/pr`** — Create the pull request.\n   176→3. **`/pr-review`** — Review and merge. Or `kspec workflow start @pr-review-merge`.\n   177→\n   178→**Quality gates (never skip without explicit approval):**\n   179→- All CI checks passing\n   180→- All review comments addressed\n   181→- All review threads resolved\n   182→- AC coverage verified\n   183→\n   184→**After merge:** `kspec task complete @ref --reason \"Merged in PR #N. Summary...\"`\n   185→\n   186→## Commit Convention\n   187→\n   188→```\n   189→feat: Feature description\n   190→\n   191→Task: @task-slug\n   192→Spec: @spec-ref\n   193→```\n   194→\n   195→Trailers enable `kspec log @ref` to find commits by task or spec.\n   196→\n   197→## Code Annotations\n   198→\n   199→Link tests to acceptance criteria:\n   200→\n   201→```typescript\n   202→// AC: @spec-item ac-N\n   203→it('should validate input', () => { ... });\n   204→```\n   205→\n   206→Every AC SHOULD have at least one test with this annotation.\n   207→\n   208→## Ralph Loop Mode\n   209→\n   210→When running in automated loop mode (ralph):\n   211→\n   212→### The Loop\n   213→\n   214→```\n   215→for each iteration:\n   216→  1. Ralph checks eligible tasks — if none, exits loop\n   217→  2. Agent works on tasks, may create PR(s)\n   218→  3. Agent stops responding (turn complete)\n   219→  4. Ralph sends reflection prompt\n   220→  5. Ralph processes pending_review via subagent\n   221→  6. Continue\n   222→```\n   223→\n   224→**When you stop responding, ralph continues automatically.** Do NOT call `end-loop` after creating a PR.\n   225→\n   226→### Task Inheritance\n   227→\n   228→Priority: `pending_review` > `in_progress` > `pending`. Always inherit existing work before starting new tasks.\n   229→\n   230→### Blocking Rules\n   231→\n   232→**Block only for genuine external blockers:**\n   233→- Requires human architectural decision\n   234→- Needs spec clarification\n   235→- Depends on external API/service not available\n   236→- Formally blocked by `depends_on`\n   237→\n   238→**Do NOT block for:**\n   239→- Task seems complex (do the work)\n   240→- Tests are failing (fix them)\n   241→- Service needs running (start it)\n   242→- Another task's PR is in CI (not a formal dependency)\n   243→\n   244→**After blocking a task:**\n   245→```bash\n   246→kspec task block @task --reason \"Reason...\"\n   247→kspec tasks ready --eligible\n   248→# If tasks returned: work on next one\n   249→# If empty: stop responding — ralph auto-exits\n   250→```\n   251→\n   252→**One blocked task is NOT \"no more work.\"** `kspec tasks ready --eligible` output is authoritative.\n   253→\n   254→## Running Services\n   255→\n   256→### Daemon\n   257→\n   258→```bash\n   259→kspec serve start                          # Foreground\n   260→kspec serve start --daemon                 # Background (daemonized)\n   261→```\n   262→\n   263→Default port 3456. Health: `GET /api/health`.\n   264→\n   265→### E2E Tests\n   266→\n   267→E2E tests **manage their own daemon** — do NOT start one manually.\n   268→\n   269→```bash\n   270→npm run test:e2e -w packages/web-ui\n   271→npm run test:e2e -w packages/web-ui -- tests/e2e/tasks.spec.ts  # Specific file\n   272→```\n   273→\n   274→**Architecture:**\n   275→- Tests in `packages/web-ui/tests/e2e/`\n   276→- Import from `../fixtures/test-base` (NOT main `tests/fixtures/`)\n   277→- Daemon runs on port 3456\n   278→- Each test gets isolated temp dir\n   279→- E2E fixtures are SEPARATE from unit test fixtures — never mix them\n   280→\n   281→```typescript\n   282→import { test, expect } from '../fixtures/test-base';\n   283→\n   284→test('my test', async ({ daemon, page }) => {\n   285→  await page.goto('http://localhost:3456');\n   286→});\n   287→```\n   288→\n   289→### Web UI Development\n   290→\n   291→```bash\n   292→npm run dev -w packages/web-ui  # Vite dev server (port 5173, connects to daemon on 3456)\n   293→```\n   294→\n   295→## Test Fixture Patterns\n   296→\n   297→### ULID Format\n   298→\n   299→ULIDs use Crockford base32 which **excludes: I, L, O, U**.\n   300→\n   301→```\n   302→❌ 01TRAIT10...  (contains I)\n   303→❌ 01MODULE0...  (contains O and U)\n   304→✅ 01TASK0000... (T, A, S, K all valid)\n   305→```\n   306→\n   307→**This causes SILENT failures** — invalid ULIDs fail schema validation but tests appear to pass because the fixture doesn't load.\n   308→\n   309→**Solution:** Use `testUlid('PREFIX')` helper or `kspec util ulid` for valid ULIDs.\n   310→\n   311→### YAML Fixtures\n   312→\n   313→**Never use `JSON.stringify()` for YAML** — use template strings or the `yaml` library's `stringify()`.\n   314→\n   315→### Test Helpers\n   316→\n   317→| Helper | Purpose |\n   318→|--------|---------|\n   319→| `setupTempFixtures()` | Copy pre-built fixtures to temp dir |\n   320→| `createTempDir()` | Create empty temp dir |\n   321→| `initGitRepo(dir)` | Initialize git with test config |\n   322→| `testUlid(prefix?)` | Generate valid test ULID |\n   323→| `testUlids(prefix, count)` | Generate multiple unique ULIDs |\n   324→| `kspec(args, cwd)` | Run CLI command, return result |\n   325→| `kspecJson<T>(args, cwd)` | Run CLI with --json, return parsed |\n   326→\n   327→See `tests/helpers/cli.ts` for full documentation.\n   328→\n   329→### Playwright Exclusion\n   330→\n   331→Playwright E2E tests must be excluded from vitest (configured in `vitest.config.ts`). Run them separately with `npm run test:e2e`.\n   332→\n   333→## CI Limitations\n   334→\n   335→| Limitation | Detail |\n   336→|------------|--------|\n   337→| File watcher tests skip in CI | GitHub Actions doesn't support recursive `fs.watch`. Run locally be